much just saw my life flash  before my eyes our labs were organized  so that all the organic chemists were on  one level and organic chemistry involves  a lot of really flammable solvents so  you can basically imagine it was just a  lab where on top of every lab desk there  was just a bunch of petrol in my mind in  that moment i thought the whole building  would just go up in flames i could just  see  a wall of flames just like exploding  towards me  it didn't the fire was actually confined  to a really small area but  that incident affected me a lot i had  ptsd for about six months i had  nightmares every single night when i  went back near that building i got this  really uncomfortable like skin crawling  feeling and it made finishing my phd  really difficult i didn't publish as  many papers as i probably could have i  took a bit longer than i should have as  well that incident really highlighted  that i love chemistry but not enough to  die because someone else screwed up so  after i graduated i didn't really know  what to do i lived in switzerland for  half a year with my partner at the time  i got really depressed i worked on my  blog i became a moderator on skincare  addiction on reddit i started teaching  at matrix education to earn some money  on the side this is a tutoring college  that does usually classes of 15. they  hire a lot of phd students and they paid  really well and about six months later  they offered me a full-time position as  a chemistry coordinator so i was in  charge of hiring teachers teaching and  writing the teaching materials so i was  writing the textbooks and inventing  experiments and so i sort of just fell  into that role and i found that i  absolutely loved it i stayed there until  i went full time with lab muffin and i  still teach there a few hours a week  where did the name lab muffin come from  this story is just not that exciting um  so i knew i wanted to do a blog i  couldn't think of a name  and so i couldn't really start doing my  blog for about four weeks and i knew i  just had to start so i settled on lab  muffin i was sitting in a lab at the  time when i set up my first blogspot  account muffin was the nickname from my  partner at the time i told myself i  could just start and then change the  name later and i just never thought up a  better name and i never got around to  changing it so the good thing about the  phrase lab muffin is that it is like a  brand new sentence which means it's very  search engine friendly so back in 2012  if you typed in lab muffin into google i  would be the first result so if you're  trying to pick an online handle and you  want to be very easily searchable i  would definitely recommend something  relatively unique and easy to spell lab  muffin worked really well for that if  you have a really distinctive name then  i would just go with your name  but that wasn't an option for me because  there were literally 27 other michelle  wongs back when i tried to sign up to  hotmail in 1997. how did your phd slash  degree help you with your career and  what did you have to learn yourself so  my phd project was on cyclic peptides i  was making them and then exploring their  uses as drugs and supreme molecular  scaffolds so that combined with my  undergrad subjects gave me a really good  background understanding of things like  chemistry pharmacology physiology and  medicinal chemistry so from that i was  really used to looking at things like  ligands and biochemical pathways  but i never really looked at anything  like retinoids specifically so i think  more importantly i developed a lot of  other skills from my phd training and  from my previous job during a typical  phd you have to defend your ideas  against people who are  kind of trying to make you break it's a  bit toxic some people cry but you kind  of get used to having to really critique  your own ideas so that you can work out  all the flaws before you go up on stage  and have to publicly defend yourself  writing the textbooks was also really  good training because the audience was  both chemistry phds and phd students who  are teaching it and the high school  students so you had to make it work for  a lot of different people when did you  realize you could go full time as a  science communicator so in 2017 i added  banner ads to my blog and that's when  financially i started making more money  than i was spending on lab muffin and  that's when it started looking like it  could potentially be a part-time job i  was starting to get over a thousand  dollars a month without a ton of effort  and i really wish i discovered this a  lot earlier on and not six years in so  yeah i guess my advice would be if  there's a hobby that you love doing see  if you can make money out of it earlier  on in late 2017 i started my youtube  channel and that's when i started to get  a lot more sponsorship opportunities and  the workload started to really ramp up  by late 2018 i was working on lab muffin  on the train to and from work and every  weekend i pretty much had no life i  worked like 70 to 80 hours a week i was  basically in lockdown before lockdown  was a thing my accountant didn't  actually believe how many hours i worked  until i showed her my records every  sunday monday which was my weekend i'd  literally get out of bed stumble across  the room to my chair and then work  straight from 9am to 1am i went out of  my room for meals and for bathroom  breaks and that was it i do not  recommend this at all i recommend trying  to figure out a way to avoid this as  early as you can by the middle of 2019 i  was making enough money from content  creation that it could be a full-time  job and i was just about to burn out  from working every single waking moment  i was constantly shallow breathing i had  a racing heart my memory was getting  really bad and i was starting to get  these like weird feelings in my legs  like i was getting little clots  so i resigned um i'm still not really  good at that whole work-life balance  thing but it has gotten a lot better so  starting out and staying motivated i  think one of the things that really kept  me going was having a nice community  around me there were a lot of other  bloggers back in the day and we all used  to comment on each other's blogs not in  like an organized way but just being  really supportive there wasn't like an  algorithm back then so we weren't really  like boosting each other's posts like  you might on instagram it was just like  a really nice supportive environment  there was also this nail polish tag that  was going around back in the day and  every week we would have artsy wednesday  where we would have a different nail art  theme and i think these sorts of  techniques still work in the age of  social media just having lots of support  and working on nice collaborative  projects i also just felt like the  content i was making just needed to be  made no one else was making this sort of  thing back in the day so things like  scientific deep dives and myth busting  in a way that's accurate but also people  could really understand  but on the other hand there was a lot of  pseudoscience going around and so i kind  of felt like  this sort of i guess duty to keep making  my sort of content how do you get  started as a cosmetic chemist is a phd  necessary was it difficult so cosmetic  chemist is a qualification and i did my  diploma for this with the institute of  personal care science it's a very  popular program that's widely recognized  in the industry you definitely don't  need a phd to do it i think you need the  equivalent of a high school certificate  it is definitely harder to understand  what's going on without some sort of  science background and you probably  won't get as deep an understanding out  of it but it is definitely doable and if  you're interested i would highly  recommend it what makes you happiest  about doing youtube and lab muffin i'm  just a bit of a massive nerd i love  understanding stuff at a really deep  level and then explaining it to other  people i also really love the whole  creative process of creating content  whether it's presentations or diagrams  or textbooks or videos what would you  say is the most important part in  translating science to the wider  population i think accuracy is obviously  really important but also empathy just  understanding what level of complexity  people can handle what analogies would  work best for explaining something and  what feels satisfying because what tends  to work really well for myth busting is  giving people a narrative that makes  more sense than the myth you have to  help replace that with the truth what  did you study to improve the delivery of  your blog posts and videos i think this  is one of those things where the best  thing you can do is just practice a lot  just make content get feedback and then  incorporate that into your next piece of  content i think teaching is also really  helpful because it's like delivering  content and getting that real-time  feedback and this doesn't have to be  formal teaching you could just try  explaining the topic you're trying to  make content about to your friends or to  your family what are the pros and cons  of writing versus videos so i started  making videos because i felt a bit  constrained by blog posts some things  are just really hard to show you really  sort of need an animation or something  moving videos do take a lot longer  though minus the research a blog post  would take about three hours whereas a  video would take 20 hours difficulties  with using social media i think the most  annoying thing about social media is  that you have to make the algorithm  happy to get any sort of reach your  posts don't get shown to your audience  unless you tick off a whole bunch of  boxes which change all the time and that  might not be the best thing for that  particular piece of content maybe you  need to have a static post with lots of  words but the algorithm wants you to  make videos social media is also pretty  intrusive it's designed to make you want  to check it all the time and so if  that's part of your work then your  work-life balance is a bit messed up  it's particularly bad because i'm in  australia and so the peak times for  social media are weird hours for me  things like 2am on the other hand i do  kind of like that the algorithm changes  all the time because i like a challenge  i have a short attention span and so i  like making lots of diverse content what  are the best and worst parts of your job  i think the best thing is that i'm quite  a creative person and so my job is just  my entire creative outlet if you get  bored of doing the same thing all the  time then this job is fantastic the  worst thing is that the harder you work  the more you're rewarded and so that is  really bad for work-life balance also  because it's like an internet job it  constantly feels really insecure like  the algorithm might screw me over at any  moment and i would no longer have a job  i don't think this is unique to making  content or being a science communicator  i've heard similar things from a lot of  people who own their own businesses and  there are also content creators and  science communicators who are doing it  as part of a full-time salary job and so  they won't have the same issues one of  the things that really helped me with  time management is an app called toggle  i use it to record how much time it took  me to do particular things and so that  way i can better estimate how long it's  going to take me to do similar things in  the future it's really good for not over  committing yourself microphones software  camera lights i'm going to take you on a  little behind the scenes tour alright so  here is my filming setup this is my blue  microphone it's a yeti x which was  gifted  that is running into a laptop on my left  in front of me i have my old s8 which is  what i film on it is currently attached  to an ice pack because it's really hot  today and it kept overheating that's  connected to a teleprompter you can see  my old phone there as well reflecting  into that this teleprompter has been  really good i've only had it for a few  months and it's made my video making so  much quicker because of the teleprompter  means i can't actually touch my phone so  my phone is attached to my tablet so  this is connected with samsung flow and  so i can control the phone through this  tablet this is my lighting situation i  have a softbox and behind that i have i  don't know this like led array that led  array lights up my background and that's  also why sometimes my face looks lighter  than the rest of my body because  the light from that gets blocked off by  the softbox i'm sure you've seen my  background a million times and the  reason there's so much clutter around  here is actually i tried to have a  really nice empty studio but it turns  out having lots of stuff around is  really good for audio quality i don't  know if anyone's noticed this but i have  a little box here that's just full of  spatulas what platform is most lucrative  for you is blogging truly dead so this  might be a bit surprising but my ad  income is actually much better from my  blog than youtube even though i spend so  much more time on youtube there are lots  of blogs that are doing way better than  me as well especially food blogs i do  think it's a little bit harder to get  established as a blog these days because  google does rank you higher if you've  got an older website so if you are  planning to start a blog then i would  recommend doing it alongside social  media because discoverability is a lot  better on social media in terms of  sponsorships i get the most money from  instagram because i can produce a lot  more sponsored instagram content than i  can with youtube videos youtube videos  take so long to make is there a point  where skincare can become obsessive or  unhealthy definitely i think a lot of  people overthink skin care it's easy to  just take the scientific evidence too  seriously in skin care a lot of it is  just not that clear-cut the evidence is  really weak and i feel like a lot of  myths come from just overthinking that  evidence whenever you see a strict rule  for anything usually that's pretty  suspect because there aren't that many  studies out there on non-medical skin  care in science you generally need lots  of replicated studies to be sure about  things and that just doesn't really  exist in skincare i think a lot of the  time getting obsessive about skin care  is more of a symptom and it's not skin  care causing the obsession a lot of  people who come to skin care come there  because of self-esteem issues and skin  care just becomes an outlet what have  you believed in the past that turned out  to be myths pretty much every older myth  i've debunked i've probably believed for  example sunscreen scattering and  reflecting uv this doesn't happen as  much anymore because over the years i've  become a lot more cynical and so now if  there's any new claims i just start off  not believing it by default so i got a  lot of questions about my future plans  and i do have some ideas for things that  i really want to branch into but i don't  want to say too much because  again i don't like mentioning things  that i'm working on because i have so  many ideas that i just don't take  through to completion i do think i  managed to do a decent amount of stuff  but there is only one of me and so it's  really hard to get everything i want  done i do not have plans for a skincare  line maybe in the future i could see  myself collaborating with someone but  i don't think i can start my own line  because i would just end up spending all  day doing stuff that i don't like in  other words just doing admin hopefully  this video was interesting and gave you  an insight into how lab muffin works it  would be really sad if i released this  video and then quit in a month but  hopefully that doesn't happen hopefully  there will be a 15-year video as well  thank you so much to everyone who's been  supporting me and allowing this to  become my full-time job especially if  you've been following since back with  the blog days back when i used to do  nail art i think it's really cool that  we've built up this nerd community over  the last decade and hopefully it keeps  going
 

